  "focus": "Class 9 is the foundation year for the boards: abstraction jumps sharply with atoms and molecules, Euclid's geometry, polynomials and the French Revolution. Students who build clean notes, learn derivations properly and practise proof-style answers in Class 9 find Class 10 dramatically easier — this is the year study habits are formed.",
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Why is Class 9 considered tough?",
      "answer": "The syllabus introduces abstract thinking — atomic structure, geometric proofs, algebra — after the gentler middle-school years. Steady daily practice closes the gap within a term."
    },
    {
      "question": "Should Class 9 students solve sample papers?",
      "answer": "Chapter-wise questions and worksheets matter more in Class 9. Save full sample papers for the final months, and start previous-paper familiarity early through chapter-wise PYQs."
    }
  ]
